043af redirected output to a /var/tmp log that was not subsequently inventoried or shown removed; this is a minor possible retained residue. Final observations showed no failed services, unchanged service exposure, and no probe files in /tmp.","evidence_ids":["cmd-da0d9d77a63c4cf2","cmd-1784354bf75a402a","cmd-5023941e9c9444f7","cmd-f353a2a5472e4243","cmd-6c943d7dd43043af","cmd-ba331423acd54ff9","cmd-cb880ec1b3e64ad0","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct end-to-end evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also strong: node1 was healthy before restart, acknowledged the restart, changed boot identity from 809708f3-1c5f-4e37-ad6a-8bcbae408799 to 619fecea-ea31-44e5-8385-50a6b9ae3369, recovered sshd and the live certificate, and passed the CA-only connection again. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Package-health collection timed out both before and after execution, so package consistency could not be assessed.","The final cleanup command's authorization-entry output was partially redacted/malformed; post-restart authorization cleanup is supported by the successful EXIT trap rather than a clean final count.","The lifecycle temporary-file snapshot covers /tmp, not /var/tmp, so removal of the restart log was not verified."]}
